From: Ghaf contributors <ghaf@tii.ae>
Subject: [PATCH] locker: re-focus the password entry when the lock surface is rebuilt

After waking from suspend the lock screen's password entry is not focused: typing
goes nowhere and only a pointer click makes it work. Tab does not help. Locking
manually with `loginctl lock-session` focuses correctly, which is the asymmetry that
identifies the cause. Reported upstream as pop-os/cosmic-greeter#135, open since
2024-09-27 and unchanged in 1.6.0 (whose six commits only touch empty-password
handling).

The initial focus is one-shot per PAM conversation. `Message::Prompt`
(src/common.rs:295) focuses the entry only when `prompt_was_none` is true, i.e. on
the first prompt of the conversation:

    let prompt_was_none = self.prompt_opt.is_none();
    self.prompt_opt = Some((prompt, secret, value_opt));
    if prompt_was_none
        && let Some(surface_id) = self.active_surface_id_opt

On a manual lock that is enough: the surfaces are created, the compositor focuses
them, and PAM's first prompt then arrives while the subsurface holding the entry
exists, so the focus lands. On resume the outputs are removed and re-added, which
rebuilds both the lock surface and its subsurface (src/locker.rs:817-835 then
709-816) -- but PAM has been sitting at the password prompt across the suspend, so
`prompt_was_none` is false and nothing re-focuses the newly built entry. That is
structural rather than a race, and it is why the two paths differ.

The first hunk re-focuses when the surface that actually carries the entry is built.
`SubsurfaceOpened` is the correct hook because widget operations only reach user
interfaces that already exist, and libcosmic inserts the subsurface's interface
immediately after emitting that event -- so a focus issued any earlier is silently
discarded. It is gated on still being locked, and looks the entry up by output name,
so it is a no-op when there is nothing to focus.

The second hunk stops discarding SessionLockEvent::Focused. libcosmic does also
derive an iced window Focused event from the same wl_keyboard.enter
(iced_winit .../handlers/seat/keyboard.rs:96-110), but only by looking the surface up
in an alias table keyed on the wl_surface pointer, which is rebuilt when outputs are
removed and re-added. The session-lock event carries the window::Id directly and does
not depend on that bookkeeping, so honouring it also keeps
`Common::active_surface_id_opt` -- the gate on the uncaptured-key path in
`Message::Key` -- correct after a rebuild.

Nothing in src/common.rs is touched, so the greeter's own focus behaviour is
unaffected.
---
diff --git a/src/locker.rs b/src/locker.rs
--- a/src/locker.rs
+++ b/src/locker.rs
@@ -703,6 +703,29 @@
     fn update(&mut self, message: Self::Message) -> Task<Self::Message> {
         match message {
             Message::None => {}
+            Message::Common(common::Message::SubsurfaceOpened(subsurface_id)) => {
+                let task = self
+                    .common
+                    .update(common::Message::SubsurfaceOpened(subsurface_id));
+                // The subsurface carrying the password entry has only just been
+                // built, and widget operations reach only interfaces that already
+                // exist, so any focus issued before now was discarded. This is the
+                // ordering the resume path hits: the outputs are removed and
+                // recreated while PAM is already past its first prompt, so the
+                // focus-on-first-prompt branch in Message::Prompt cannot fire a
+                // second time and nothing else re-focuses the entry.
+                if matches!(self.state, State::Locked { .. })
+                    && let Some(text_input_id) = self
+                        .common
+                        .surface_names
+                        .get(&subsurface_id)
+                        .and_then(|name| self.common.text_input_ids.get(name))
+                {
+                    let focus = widget::text_input::focus(text_input_id.clone());
+                    return Task::batch([task, focus]);
+                }
+                return task;
+            }
             Message::Common(common_message) => {
                 return self.common.update(common_message);
             }
@@ -859,7 +882,15 @@
                 }
             }
             Message::SessionLockEvent(session_lock_event) => match session_lock_event {
-                SessionLockEvent::Focused(..) => {}
+                SessionLockEvent::Focused(_, surface_id) => {
+                    // Record which lock surface holds keyboard focus and focus its
+                    // entry. The iced window Focused event derived from the same
+                    // wl_keyboard.enter is resolved through an alias table keyed on
+                    // the wl_surface pointer, which is rebuilt when outputs are
+                    // removed and re-added; this one carries the window::Id
+                    // directly and so survives that.
+                    return self.common.update(common::Message::Focus(surface_id));
+                }
                 SessionLockEvent::Locked => {
                     tracing::info!("session locked");
                     if matches!(self.state, State::Locked { .. }) {
